00:34:50.140 --> 00:34:52.176
and then what the reality was.


00:34:53.018 --> 00:34:54.383
Not yet.


00:34:54.395 --> 00:34:55.885
We were just rehearsing.


00:34:55.896 --> 00:34:56.806
Sit down.


00:34:56.814 --> 00:34:57.644
- What are you doing--


00:34:57.648 --> 00:34:59.263
- Sit down, you little.


00:34:59.274 --> 00:35:01.811
And obviously with what
happened in their lives


00:35:01.819 --> 00:35:04.185
with John being arrested,


00:35:04.196 --> 00:35:06.733
I think that's sort of like
the culmination of it all,


00:35:06.740 --> 00:35:08.605
and along the way I'm sure


00:35:08.617 --> 00:35:09.777
that in their relationship,


00:35:09.785 --> 00:35:13.369
there were moments where
she was very much in love,


00:35:13.372 --> 00:35:17.832
but wondering, he's incredibly
driven and ambitious,


00:35:17.835 --> 00:35:20.247
and how does she fit into that picture?


00:35:20.254 --> 00:35:21.790
- Eventually the car will get built,


00:35:21.797 --> 00:35:25.085
but right now I'm trying to build a brand.


00:35:25.092 --> 00:35:27.549
- Well, it concerns me


00:35:27.553 --> 00:35:30.010
where we're gonna raise the children.


00:35:30.013 --> 00:35:32.800
- Well, wherever we set up shop,


00:35:32.808 --> 00:35:35.220
we won't have to spend much time there.


00:35:35.227 --> 00:35:36.637
All that matters is which government


00:35:36.645 --> 00:35:37.760
gives us the most money.


00:35:39.314 --> 00:35:40.520
- Well, it matters to me,


00:35:42.234 --> 00:35:43.565
and it matters to the kids.


00:35:47.406 --> 00:35:48.191
- Don't worry.


00:35:49.241 --> 00:35:50.902
It's gonna be great for all of us.


00:36:00.461 --> 00:36:02.873
-  Northern Ireland's
now experiencing rioting


00:36:02.880 --> 00:36:06.247
on a scale not seen for many years.


00:36:06.258 --> 00:36:08.374
- Why pick Belfast, Ireland,


00:36:08.385 --> 00:36:10.125
to build your motor plant?


00:36:10.137 --> 00:36:11.297
- Oh, it's very simple.


00:36:11.305 --> 00:36:13.011
Nobody anywhere in the world


00:36:13.015 --> 00:36:15.552
would come up with the
financing we needed.


00:36:15.559 --> 00:36:17.800
Now, nobody else would go to Belfast


00:36:17.811 --> 00:36:19.347
because it was such a terrible,


00:36:19.354 --> 00:36:21.219
dangerous place to be.


00:36:21.231 --> 00:36:22.687
- I read in the newspapers


00:36:22.691 --> 00:36:24.397
that John DeLorean had secured


00:36:24.401 --> 00:36:28.064
the investment he needed to
establish his car project


00:36:28.071 --> 00:36:30.232
in Northern Ireland against the background


00:36:30.240 --> 00:36:32.856
of the famous troubles


00:36:32.868 --> 00:36:35.029
that was rife at that time.


00:36:35.871 --> 00:36:37.862
-  Prolonged civil
unrest between Catholics


00:36:37.873 --> 00:36:40.580
and Protestants scares off industrialists,


00:36:40.584 --> 00:36:42.791
depriving Northern Ireland of jobs,


00:36:42.794 --> 00:36:45.410
which it needs more than
any other area of Britain.


00:36:46.340 --> 00:36:47.796
- The unemployment was insane.


00:36:47.799 --> 00:36:49.380
It was 30, 40%,


00:36:49.384 --> 00:36:53.593
and John had something to offer, jobs.


00:36:53.597 --> 00:36:56.179
The Labor government
in Britain at the time


00:36:56.183 --> 00:36:59.767
was willing to put in subsidies, loans,


00:36:59.770 --> 00:37:01.681
a piece of land,


00:37:01.688 --> 00:37:03.519
but here's the catch.


00:37:03.524 --> 00:37:04.934
It's got a population


00:37:04.942 --> 00:37:07.183
that has never built a car before.


00:37:07.194 --> 00:37:09.185
There's no pre-existing structure


00:37:09.196 --> 00:37:10.732
to build a car company there,


00:37:10.739 --> 00:37:13.697
and you have to do it in two years.


00:37:13.700 --> 00:37:16.112
I mean, are you kidding me?


00:37:16.119 --> 00:37:17.234
And all they have at this point


00:37:17.246 --> 00:37:19.282
is a hand built prototype,


00:37:19.289 --> 00:37:22.781
so it was a long list
of things to accomplish


00:37:22.793 --> 00:37:25.660
to eventually be able to
productionize the car.


00:37:25.671 --> 00:37:27.707
It became clear to everyone


00:37:27.714 --> 00:37:29.375
that they needed to have help


00:37:29.383 --> 00:37:30.839
to weed out all the problems


00:37:30.842 --> 00:37:33.800
and actually engineer this
car to be mass produced.


00:37:33.804 --> 00:37:37.046
Well, Colin Chapman
comes into the picture.


00:37:40.519 --> 00:37:42.851
- Probably the finest automobile engineer


00:37:42.854 --> 00:37:45.186
I have ever met in my life, Colin Chapman.


00:37:45.190 --> 00:37:46.771
I'll introduce Colin now.


00:37:47.901 --> 00:37:50.984
-  Colin Chapman is
the founder of Lotus Cars.


00:37:50.988 --> 00:37:53.946
He was a pioneer in the racing industry.


00:37:53.949 --> 00:37:56.190
He dominated Formula One.


00:37:56.201 --> 00:38:00.570
-  A deal had been struck
with Lotus Cars as a company


00:38:00.581 --> 00:38:03.414
and Colin Chapman as a named individual


00:38:03.417 --> 00:38:06.409
to engineer and develop DeLorean cars


00:38:06.420 --> 00:38:08.001
that could enter production.


00:38:08.005 --> 00:38:10.246
- Well, the idea of working with Lotus


00:38:10.257 --> 00:38:11.793
sounded like a good idea.


00:38:11.800 --> 00:38:12.915
We would all get together,


00:38:12.926 --> 00:38:15.212
and we'd become a joint organization


00:38:15.220 --> 00:38:19.054
and go finish the production design work.


00:38:19.057 --> 00:38:21.969
- Well, when we first got to Lotus,


00:38:21.977 --> 00:38:23.513
I mean, right out of the chute,


00:38:23.520 --> 00:38:24.760
it was kind of awkward.


00:38:24.771 --> 00:38:28.730
Here Bill and I and some
others had spent years


00:38:28.734 --> 00:38:30.725
getting us to this point,


00:38:30.736 --> 00:38:34.194
and then now it was like
Bill Collins and Bob Manion


00:38:34.197 --> 00:38:35.983
and whoever else was involved,


00:38:35.991 --> 00:38:39.904
they can kind of like just watch us,


00:38:42.080 --> 00:38:43.820
and that of course isn't
what I was gonna do,


00:38:43.832 --> 00:38:46.665
and it certainly wasn't gonna
be what Bill was gonna do.


00:38:46.668 --> 00:38:47.783
- This is Bill.


00:38:47.794 --> 00:38:49.125
Johnin?


00:38:49.129 --> 00:38:50.790
-  Bill, how the hell are you?


00:38:50.797 --> 00:38:53.083
- Look, John, I got the
contract you sent over,


00:38:54.092 --> 00:38:57.209
and I have to say it
raises some questions.


00:38:58.597 --> 00:39:00.963
You've got a lot in
here about Lotus, John,


00:39:00.974 --> 00:39:02.635
but nothing about me.


00:39:02.643 --> 00:39:03.473
- Oh, you don't have to
worry about that, Bill.


00:39:03.477 --> 00:39:05.763
It's just a lot of paperwork.


00:39:05.771 --> 00:39:07.181
You know what you're doing.


00:39:07.189 --> 00:39:08.019
- I understand that, John,


00:39:08.023 --> 00:39:09.354
but I thought this was supposed


00:39:09.358 --> 00:39:11.599
to be a collaboration on the redesign,


00:39:11.610 --> 00:39:13.316
but Chapman's nowhere to be found.


00:39:14.738 --> 00:39:16.899
- Well, they're doing a lot
of R and D, aren't they?


00:39:16.907 --> 00:39:18.568
- That's the other thing.


00:39:18.575 --> 00:39:22.909
This company in here, this GPD,


00:39:22.913 --> 00:39:24.278
I've never even heard of it.


00:39:25.332 --> 00:39:26.663
- You don't need to
worry about that, Bill.


00:39:26.667 --> 00:39:30.080
It's not going to affect you at all, okay?


00:39:30.087 --> 00:39:32.203
- Well, I don't see how that's possible.


00:39:32.214 --> 00:39:33.795
They got a hefty line item


00:39:33.799 --> 00:39:34.914
in here on engineering,


00:39:34.925 --> 00:39:36.961
but that's what we're
supposed to be doing.


00:39:36.968 --> 00:39:38.458
It just seems like you're paying twice


00:39:38.470 --> 00:39:40.461
for the same thing, John.


00:39:41.556 --> 00:39:44.093
- Well, it must be some
kind of a tax shelter, Bill.


00:39:44.101 --> 00:39:44.931
I don't know.


00:39:44.935 --> 00:39:45.765
I'm not a CPA.


00:39:45.769 --> 00:39:46.554
I'm sorry.


00:39:48.897 --> 00:39:50.262
- John, what am I doing here?


00:39:52.150 --> 00:39:53.014
Why am I even here?


00:39:58.824 --> 00:39:59.813
- I liked Bill.


00:39:59.825 --> 00:40:01.656
I think he'd done a tremendous job,


00:40:01.660 --> 00:40:05.619
but the moment I found
him reading the contract,


00:40:05.622 --> 00:40:09.240
it was quite clear that
what he'd already decided


00:40:09.251 --> 00:40:11.617
in his own mind was true,


00:40:11.628 --> 00:40:13.118
that he was out of a job.


00:40:13.130 --> 00:40:16.418
Colin Chapman was not gonna have anybody


00:40:16.425 --> 00:40:19.087
looking over Colin Chapman's shoulder.


00:40:19.094 --> 00:40:20.459
-  To do business like this,


00:40:20.470 --> 00:40:22.176
don't you have to be pretty ruthless?


00:40:24.725 --> 00:40:26.135
- I don't think you have to be ruthless.


00:40:26.143 --> 00:40:27.724
I think you have to be prepared to make


00:40:27.728 --> 00:40:30.344
some unpalatable decisions at times


00:40:30.355 --> 00:40:32.892
because frequently you're
faced with making a decision


00:40:32.899 --> 00:40:34.230
between two evils,


00:40:34.234 --> 00:40:36.145
and you're gonna hurt somebody.


00:40:36.153 --> 00:40:39.065
- John DeLorean himself, he knew.


00:40:39.072 --> 00:40:40.187
He'd arranged with Chapman


00:40:40.198 --> 00:40:42.905
that this is the way it's gonna be.


00:40:42.909 --> 00:40:46.822
Nobody had told the
guys from the U.S. team.


00:40:46.830 --> 00:40:49.492
-  It becomes
painfully clear that Bill


00:40:49.499 --> 00:40:52.081
and his engineering team are out of a job,


00:40:52.085 --> 00:40:53.450
and not only that,


00:40:53.462 --> 00:40:55.498
there's something very fishy going on


00:40:55.505 --> 00:40:57.496
with the finances of this deal.


00:40:57.507 --> 00:41:00.624
Colin Chapman was gonna
be willing to do things


00:41:00.635 --> 00:41:03.422
that Bill would never have
been comfortable with,


00:41:03.430 --> 00:41:06.547
and for John, I think that
was an important aspect


00:41:06.558 --> 00:41:07.889
to this partnership,


00:41:07.893 --> 00:41:08.928
and it was the type of thing


00:41:08.935 --> 00:41:10.596
that was so important to him at the time


00:41:10.604 --> 00:41:13.266
that he was willing to sacrifice Bill.


00:41:21.281 --> 00:41:24.739
- Dear, John, after much
deliberation on my part,


00:41:24.743 --> 00:41:26.358
I feel it is obvious to both of us


00:41:26.369 --> 00:41:28.325
that my participation in your venture


00:41:28.330 --> 00:41:30.161
is no longer the same as intended.


00:41:32.459 --> 00:41:33.915
Under the current circumstances,


00:41:33.919 --> 00:41:36.035
I feel the only alternative for me


00:41:36.046 --> 00:41:37.877
is to tender my resignation


00:41:37.881 --> 00:41:40.873
in all my present positions at this time.


00:41:40.884 --> 00:41:43.466
I wish you and the rest of
the team the best of luck.


00:41:45.847 --> 00:41:47.053
All these years later,


00:41:48.391 --> 00:41:49.597
it still looks great,


00:41:50.977 --> 00:41:53.218
and it would be nice to
have a brown interior.


00:41:55.941 --> 00:41:58.353
Well, it'd been a long, long time


00:41:58.360 --> 00:42:00.442
that we'd been working on the project,


00:42:00.445 --> 00:42:05.405
and I felt that I was being
totally undercut by John,


00:42:05.784 --> 00:42:07.615
and he really owed me a hell of lot more


00:42:07.619 --> 00:42:09.155
than to treat me the way he did,


00:42:10.330 --> 00:42:12.446
and itjust seemed like
the time to move on.


00:42:14.292 --> 00:42:16.533
-  Do you remember
having the conversation


00:42:16.545 --> 00:42:17.785
with John where you said,


00:42:17.796 --> 00:42:18.626
"I'm resigning?"


00:42:18.630 --> 00:42:20.495
- No, I don't remember.


00:42:21.633 --> 00:42:24.500
I think he was happy to see me go.


00:42:24.511 --> 00:42:25.466
-  Do you really?


00:42:25.470 --> 00:42:26.300
- Yeah.


00:42:26.304 --> 00:42:27.384
-  He didn't
try to stop you at all?


00:42:27.389 --> 00:42:28.174
- No.


00:42:33.895 --> 00:42:35.055
We're all males,


00:42:35.063 --> 00:42:36.143
but there's something to be said


00:42:36.147 --> 00:42:37.603
about being the guy that's sort of the guy


00:42:38.733 --> 00:42:41.395
that bore the baby, as it were,


00:42:41.403 --> 00:42:42.688
with the DMC-12.


00:43:01.840 --> 00:43:03.296
- Before we started filming,


00:43:03.300 --> 00:43:07.760
we knew nothing about
John DeLorean really.


00:43:12.142 --> 00:43:13.803
We basically came to the project


00:43:13.810 --> 00:43:16.267
because a British television station


00:43:16.271 --> 00:43:19.809
wanted to do something on Ireland.


00:43:19.816 --> 00:43:21.352
- And I was thinking, oh, boy,


00:43:24.905 --> 00:43:27.317
They said, "No, no, we want
it to be about automobiles


00:43:27.324 --> 00:43:28.530
"because everybody in England


00:43:28.533 --> 00:43:30.398
"just loves automobiles."


00:43:30.410 --> 00:43:32.275
-  Like most of our films,


00:43:32.287 --> 00:43:34.573
the idea was to kind of watch somebody


00:43:34.581 --> 00:43:37.163
who wants to fulfill a life dream,


00:43:37.167 --> 00:43:39.658
and that's who John was.


00:43:39.669 --> 00:43:43.036
-  John Zachary DeLorean
certainly doesn't smile much.


00:43:43.048 --> 00:43:44.254
The reason is simple.


00:43:44.257 --> 00:43:46.248
The most important project in his life


00:43:46.259 --> 00:43:47.840
is yet to be accomplished.


00:43:47.844 --> 00:43:49.380
- He was very guarded


00:43:49.387 --> 00:43:54.006
because he had so many
things, I guess, on his mind


00:43:54.017 --> 00:43:56.599
to get figured out all the time.


00:43:56.603 --> 00:43:59.845
- We've had some kind of
criticism right from day one.


00:43:59.856 --> 00:44:03.098
First, we were never gonna
get the program together.


00:44:03.109 --> 00:44:04.770
Then we'd never get the financing.


00:44:04.778 --> 00:44:06.234
Then we'd never build the plant.


00:44:06.237 --> 00:44:10.321
- I don't think the motor
car manufacturing industries,


00:44:10.325 --> 00:44:13.488
whether it be America, UK, mainland,


00:44:13.495 --> 00:44:16.703
actually ever thought
that it could be done.


00:44:16.706 --> 00:44:19.288
- Now the critics are
forecasting our failure


00:44:19.292 --> 00:44:20.702
two or three years from now,


00:44:20.710 --> 00:44:22.325
so I think as long as you can


00:44:22.337 --> 00:44:23.702
always keep your failure two


00:44:23.713 --> 00:44:24.998
or three years in the future,


00:44:25.006 --> 00:44:26.587
you're probably gonna do okay.


00:44:26.591 --> 00:44:28.252
- I think DeLorean's problem


00:44:28.259 --> 00:44:30.375
was at that time he needed cars,


00:44:30.387 --> 00:44:31.752
and he needed cars in a hurry.


00:44:31.763 --> 00:44:34.379
- You got to pick the
three or four big markets


00:44:35.225 --> 00:44:37.716
We can't wait 14 months to start selling.


00:44:37.727 --> 00:44:42.346
- It was a constant panic
of racing the clock,


00:44:42.357 --> 00:44:44.814
and it was damn the torpedoes


00:44:44.818 --> 00:44:47.025
and full speed ahead.


00:44:47.028 --> 00:44:49.485
-  It was such a
process of building the car


00:44:49.489 --> 00:44:51.025
and seeing what was working


00:44:51.032 --> 00:44:52.738
and what wasn't working.


00:44:52.742 --> 00:44:54.653
- We've had problems on
the upper control arm


00:44:54.661 --> 00:44:55.992
around the ball joint,


00:44:55.996 --> 00:44:59.113
but we also had a torsion
rod failure at 41,000 cycles.


00:44:59.124 --> 00:45:01.911
- Everybody knew that there
were gonna be problems.


00:45:01.918 --> 00:45:03.533
The view you took was, well,


00:45:03.545 --> 00:45:04.751
yeah, we expected this.


00:45:04.754 --> 00:45:06.244
Fine, let's get on and fix it.


00:45:07.298 --> 00:45:09.710
- It was crazy, absolutely crazy,


00:45:09.718 --> 00:45:11.174
but it was exciting.


00:45:11.177 --> 00:45:12.462
You were an operator on the floor


00:45:12.470 --> 00:45:14.586
seeing that lovely stainless steel car


00:45:14.597 --> 00:45:16.212
going together the way it should go.


00:45:16.224 --> 00:45:17.680
That was very rewarding.


00:45:18.601 --> 00:45:22.219
- You felt part of a family
when you went in there.


00:45:22.230 --> 00:45:24.266
There was absolutely no trouble,


00:45:24.274 --> 00:45:26.481
no Protestant, Catholic divide.


00:45:26.484 --> 00:45:28.850
- One of the proudest
achievements, I think,


00:45:28.862 --> 00:45:32.980
of DeLorean is that it was
a form of social experiment.


00:45:32.991 --> 00:45:35.607
You had for the first
time in Northern Ireland


00:45:35.618 --> 00:45:36.949
Catholics and Protestants


00:45:36.953 --> 00:45:38.864
working alongside each other.


00:45:38.872 --> 00:45:41.409
The social experiment
was beginning to work.


00:45:41.416 --> 00:45:44.704
You can have a mixed work
force and be successful.


00:45:44.711 --> 00:45:48.420
- In two years and 11 months,


00:45:48.423 --> 00:45:49.788
we had built a factory.


00:45:50.925 --> 00:45:53.211
We had designed a car.


00:45:53.219 --> 00:45:54.550
The cars were built,


00:45:54.554 --> 00:45:57.671
and that was the best time


00:45:57.682 --> 00:46:00.173
I've ever had working in any company.


00:46:01.811 --> 00:46:05.929
-  When the first
six cars came rolling out,


00:46:05.940 --> 00:46:07.476
the news crews were all there.


00:46:08.443 --> 00:46:09.774
John was the biggest hero


00:46:09.778 --> 00:46:11.143
in Northern Ireland that day.


00:46:12.155 --> 00:46:14.771
- It's incredible that he got 3,500 cars


00:46:14.783 --> 00:46:16.865
into the United States on a boat,


00:46:16.868 --> 00:46:18.984
and we actually sailed
on the damn boat too


00:46:19.913 --> 00:46:23.952
because I just was so
excited to see that happen


00:46:23.958 --> 00:46:28.201
'cause I knew he showed
them that he could do it.


00:46:28.213 --> 00:46:29.043
-  Buyers are reported to be


00:46:29.047 --> 00:46:30.537
queuing up in the United States


00:46:30.548 --> 00:46:33.415
for the company's
futuristic Goldwing DMC-12.


00:46:33.426 --> 00:46:36.133
-  And D.A. and Chris,


00:46:36.137 --> 00:46:37.217
you watch their movie,


00:46:37.222 --> 00:46:39.053
and the perception you get at the end


00:46:39.057 --> 00:46:40.968
is this is a success.


00:46:40.975 --> 00:46:42.886
- Everything was hunky dOI‘Y:


00:46:42.894 --> 00:46:44.725
and it was great, you know?


00:46:44.729 --> 00:46:46.890
We'd succeeded in October '81.


00:46:54.114 --> 00:46:55.854
-  How is it?


00:46:55.865 --> 00:46:59.028
- DeLorean is four or five little moves


00:46:59.035 --> 00:47:00.821
away from victory.


00:47:02.205 --> 00:47:04.116
He's on the doorstep of that victory.


00:47:06.084 --> 00:47:08.826
In the music of it, it's like Bolero.


00:47:10.213 --> 00:47:11.419
John just says .


00:47:16.261 --> 00:47:17.842
We don't migrate from that.


00:47:18.763 --> 00:47:21.004
He's played a pretty high stakes game,


00:47:21.015 --> 00:47:23.677
and he really, really stays calm


00:47:23.685 --> 00:47:25.676
and very contained,


00:47:25.687 --> 00:47:28.269
and the drama is in him being contained


00:47:28.273 --> 00:47:30.810
while everything collapsed around him.


00:47:31.985 --> 00:47:34.192
- Things started to go south very quickly.


00:47:35.155 --> 00:47:40.024
Around the fall of 1981,


00:47:41.286 --> 00:47:43.698
there was a series of monumental cockups.


00:47:43.705 --> 00:47:45.912
-  So everything's going
really smoothly, but it's--


00:47:45.915 --> 00:47:47.951
- Well, I wouldn't say smoothly.


00:47:47.959 --> 00:47:50.291
I don't want to give the appearance


00:47:50.295 --> 00:47:53.879
that everything's just absolutely perfect.


00:47:54.841 --> 00:47:57.503
- When those cars first came off the boat,


00:47:57.510 --> 00:48:00.377
they were just a product
warranty nightmare.


00:48:00.388 --> 00:48:01.343
- The door won't close.


00:48:01.347 --> 00:48:02.553
The gaps are all wrong.


00:48:02.557 --> 00:48:04.468
The window wiper won't work.


00:48:04.475 --> 00:48:06.841
The door mechanism won't work.


00:48:06.853 --> 00:48:07.683
That happened.


00:48:07.687 --> 00:48:09.348
It happened to me once,
getting stuck in the car.


00:48:09.355 --> 00:48:11.437
Couldn't open the door, you know?


00:48:12.817 --> 00:48:14.102
- We reviewed the car.


00:48:15.111 --> 00:48:17.193
It COStS more than a corvette.


00:48:17.197 --> 00:48:18.437
It didn't handle that great.


00:48:18.448 --> 00:48:19.858
It was kind of slow.


00:48:19.866 --> 00:48:23.654
-  It was not the car he promised,


00:48:23.661 --> 00:48:27.995
this fuel efficient, high performance car.


00:48:27.999 --> 00:48:29.864
- It started to go south


00:48:29.876 --> 00:48:32.913
when this happened in the teeth


00:48:32.921 --> 00:48:36.584
of a diminishing economy.


00:48:36.591 --> 00:48:38.252
-  Now the dream seems to be fading.


00:48:38.259 --> 00:48:40.716
Sales are way down in the United States


00:48:40.720 --> 00:48:42.381
perhaps for economic reasons,


00:48:42.388 --> 00:48:45.346
perhaps because of some
dissatisfaction with the car.


00:48:45.350 --> 00:48:48.558
- In a scenario where
there's a few thousand cars


00:48:48.561 --> 00:48:50.051
coming to the U.S.,


00:48:50.063 --> 00:48:52.145
there's these quality control issues.


00:48:52.148 --> 00:48:55.106
The lesson that should
have been learned from this


00:48:55.109 --> 00:48:57.976
is let's slow production
down a little bit more.


00:48:57.987 --> 00:48:59.067
Let's figure out a way


00:48:59.072 --> 00:49:00.903
to work out all these issues.


00:49:00.907 --> 00:49:03.694
Unfortunately, that's the opposite


00:49:03.701 --> 00:49:05.362
of what John decided to do.


00:49:05.370 --> 00:49:08.783
He pulled the trigger on
basically doubling production.


00:49:08.790 --> 00:49:10.280
-  And what happened?


00:49:10.291 --> 00:49:15.251
These cars were backing up
like plague rats on the docks.


00:49:15.797 --> 00:49:18.539
- And it was just the wrong, wrong move,


00:49:18.549 --> 00:49:19.664
so the question has to be,


00:49:19.676 --> 00:49:21.541
well, why did he do this?


00:49:21.552 --> 00:49:22.917
It was a typical John move


00:49:22.929 --> 00:49:25.841
because he saw a little angle


00:49:25.848 --> 00:49:28.806
in the contract where
if you upped the amount


00:49:28.810 --> 00:49:31.802
of people that were working
there and increased jobs,


00:49:31.813 --> 00:49:32.643
then the government was


00:49:32.647 --> 00:49:35.104
supposed to be giving
him an infusion of cash,


00:49:35.108 --> 00:49:36.769
and so he thought he
was gonna take advantage


00:49:36.776 --> 00:49:38.357
of this little clause.


00:49:38.361 --> 00:49:40.647
- Anyone who deals with
the government knows


00:49:40.655 --> 00:49:43.362
that that's a risk in itself.


00:49:43.366 --> 00:49:44.731
What if the politics change?


00:49:44.742 --> 00:49:46.698
What if the administration changes?


00:49:47.620 --> 00:49:50.487
Well, Margaret Thatcher happened.


00:49:52.292 --> 00:49:54.283
- We'd had a change of government by now.


00:49:54.294 --> 00:49:56.205
The Conservatives were in power.


00:49:56.212 --> 00:50:00.797
Margaret Thatcher, the arch
privateer, was prime minister.


00:50:00.800 --> 00:50:03.416
Between Margaret Thatcher
as prime minister and us,


00:50:03.428 --> 00:50:04.759
there was a secretary of state


00:50:04.762 --> 00:50:07.424
for Northern Ireland, James Prior.


00:50:07.432 --> 00:50:10.014
- Well, I suppose my role with DeLorean


00:50:10.018 --> 00:50:12.976
was to keep him under control.


00:50:12.979 --> 00:50:16.437
The Labor government had started
off the DeLorean business.


00:50:16.441 --> 00:50:19.729
The Conservative government
took a tougher line


00:50:19.736 --> 00:50:24.321
towards subsidies for Northern
Ireland, for DeLorean.


00:50:24.324 --> 00:50:26.485
-  DeLorean wanted
another $76 million


00:50:26.492 --> 00:50:27.902
from the British.


00:50:27.910 --> 00:50:30.242
If not, he said, "There would be layoffs."


00:50:30.246 --> 00:50:32.828
- I don't care about the
British taxpayers' money


00:50:32.832 --> 00:50:33.662
or my own money.


00:50:33.666 --> 00:50:34.872
I think the most important thing


00:50:34.876 --> 00:50:36.787
is we have some people there.


00:50:36.794 --> 00:50:39.410
This is a very, very
important part of their life,


00:50:39.422 --> 00:50:41.128
and nothing in the world


00:50:41.132 --> 00:50:43.043
should be permitted to interfere with it.


00:50:43.051 --> 00:50:45.918
- John overestimated the fact


00:50:45.928 --> 00:50:49.011
that we had created jobs


00:50:49.015 --> 00:50:52.007
and that it was the jobs that would secure


00:50:53.227 --> 00:50:56.139
even more money to keep us alive.


00:50:56.147 --> 00:50:58.889
That was not the way
Margaret Thatcher saw it.


00:50:58.900 --> 00:51:01.141
- She genuinely took the view


00:51:01.152 --> 00:51:02.392
that we couldn't go on


00:51:02.403 --> 00:51:04.689
pouring a lot of money after bad,


00:51:05.656 --> 00:51:07.271
and in the end, we had to say no.


00:51:08.743 --> 00:51:09.949
- The result?


00:51:09.952 --> 00:51:11.237
We ran out of cash.


00:51:12.246 --> 00:51:15.329
Therefore, we were heading for insolvency,


00:51:16.292 --> 00:51:17.702
and that's at the point


00:51:17.710 --> 00:51:20.167
when things got very serious indeed.


00:51:23.841 --> 00:51:25.547
-  We have a group right now


00:51:25.551 --> 00:51:26.711
that has got the ability


00:51:26.719 --> 00:51:30.052
to come up with for sure 30 million


00:51:30.056 --> 00:51:31.762
and upwards of there.


00:51:33.518 --> 00:51:35.679
- And during this whole process,


00:51:35.686 --> 00:51:38.052
John's back was really
up against the wall.


00:51:38.064 --> 00:51:39.224
He had to figure out a way


00:51:39.232 --> 00:51:40.688
to pay some of the money back


00:51:40.691 --> 00:51:41.521
to the British government


00:51:41.526 --> 00:51:43.187
as well as present to them


00:51:43.194 --> 00:51:45.685
a feasible way to keep the company open


00:51:45.696 --> 00:51:47.186
and make it profitable,


00:51:47.198 --> 00:51:48.688
or else they were threatening


00:51:51.202 --> 00:51:52.612
liquidate the assets,


00:51:52.620 --> 00:51:54.406
and everyone was gonna be out of a job.


00:51:54.414 --> 00:51:55.995
-  Today DeLorean said he is looking


00:51:55.998 --> 00:51:57.408
for outside financing.


00:51:57.417 --> 00:51:59.248
-  All I want to do
is keep the factory open


00:51:59.252 --> 00:52:01.163
so that people can work, nothing else.


00:52:01.170 --> 00:52:02.910
-  You last said that
you'd keep the factory open


00:52:02.922 --> 00:52:04.287
if it was your last breath.


00:52:04.298 --> 00:52:05.504
Do you still stand by that?


00:52:05.508 --> 00:52:06.668
-  I'm still trying.


00:52:06.676 --> 00:52:08.758
- During the months of
sort of August, September,


00:52:08.761 --> 00:52:13.004
we'd had periodic contact
with John DeLorean.


00:52:13.015 --> 00:52:17.759
He on a regular basis was coming
up with potential funders.


00:52:17.770 --> 00:52:19.055
-  It's been a
lot of time and effort,


00:52:19.063 --> 00:52:20.178
but I think we're now just


00:52:20.189 --> 00:52:24.899
starting out on a very
successful relationship.


00:52:24.902 --> 00:52:25.732
-  Oh, I think so, too.


00:52:25.736 --> 00:52:27.226
-  It just took a
while to put it together,


00:52:27.238 --> 00:52:28.068
and that's all.


00:52:28.072 --> 00:52:31.815
- The workers and the
people closest to him at DMC


00:52:31.826 --> 00:52:32.656
were being told,


00:52:32.660 --> 00:52:34.525
"Hey, listen, everything's gonna be fine.


00:52:34.537 --> 00:52:37.449
"I definitely have the
sources coming through."


00:52:37.457 --> 00:52:40.699
- And we were still at the stage


00:52:40.710 --> 00:52:42.951
where we were hoping and hoping


00:52:42.962 --> 00:52:44.793
that it would go all right.


00:52:44.797 --> 00:52:46.537
-  In terms of having that,


00:52:46.549 --> 00:52:48.164
the first 10 million,


00:52:48.176 --> 00:52:51.293
it sounds like that's
gonna be Tuesday, right?


00:52:51.304 --> 00:52:52.384
-  I would say I should be able


00:52:52.388 --> 00:52:54.174
to get that going very quick.


00:52:54.182 --> 00:52:55.968
-  The impression
we got at that time


00:52:55.975 --> 00:52:56.964
was that John had pretty well


00:52:56.976 --> 00:53:01.891
gotten the money by the
17th of October, 1982.


00:53:24.504 --> 00:53:25.289
- Hello?


00:53:29.342 --> 00:53:30.127
What?


00:53:39.352 --> 00:53:42.059
What are you talking about?


00:53:42.063 --> 00:53:46.056
- We were in the city in
the apartment at 8:34.


00:53:47.527 --> 00:53:48.482
The phone rings.


00:53:49.987 --> 00:53:51.102
Mom, who was that?


00:53:52.406 --> 00:53:55.944
And one phone call,


00:53:57.411 --> 00:53:59.197
my life changed forever.


00:54:00.289 --> 00:54:02.154
-  John DeLorean
was arrested yesterday


00:54:02.166 --> 00:54:04.327
after he met with undercover drug agents


00:54:04.335 --> 00:54:05.666
who DeLorean apparently thought


00:54:05.670 --> 00:54:06.625
were going to help him make


00:54:06.629 --> 00:54:08.995
a huge profit in a cocaine deal


00:54:09.006 --> 00:54:11.668
involving more than 100 kilos of cocaine.


00:54:11.676 --> 00:54:13.837
DeLorean's wife, Cristina Ferrare,


00:54:13.844 --> 00:54:14.879
an actress and model,


00:54:14.887 --> 00:54:17.094
arrived in Los Angeles early this morning.


00:54:17.098 --> 00:54:17.928
- Wait a minute.


00:54:17.932 --> 00:54:18.967
Wait a minute.


00:54:18.975 --> 00:54:21.011
I just found out a few hours ago.


00:54:21.018 --> 00:54:22.133
I know nothing.


00:54:22.144 --> 00:54:23.304
I caught a plane.


00:54:23.312 --> 00:54:24.142
I'm here.


00:54:24.146 --> 00:54:24.976
I'm--


00:54:24.981 --> 00:54:26.471
- Everybody was in total shock.


00:54:26.482 --> 00:54:29.349
Nowhere in our wildest dreams did we think


00:54:29.360 --> 00:54:31.146
anything like this would occur.


00:54:31.153 --> 00:54:33.018
-  John DeLorean
may be the perfect proof


00:54:33.030 --> 00:54:33.985
of the old saying,


00:54:33.990 --> 00:54:35.070
"The bigger they come,


00:54:35.074 --> 00:54:36.564
"the harder they fall,"


00:54:36.576 --> 00:54:37.406
and we might add,


00:54:37.410 --> 00:54:39.492
"the more people want to hear about it."


00:54:39.495 --> 00:54:40.701
- It was just disbelief,


00:54:42.248 --> 00:54:45.240
and then the news just progressively


00:54:45.251 --> 00:54:46.616
got worse and worse and worse.


00:54:46.627 --> 00:54:50.415
- He was expressing an
interest in financing


00:54:50.423 --> 00:54:52.084
some type of operation


00:54:52.091 --> 00:54:55.925
that would produce quickly
large sums of money


00:54:55.928 --> 00:54:58.169
on return from the investment.


00:54:58.180 --> 00:55:01.513
- I was shocked out of my socks


00:55:02.476 --> 00:55:05.684
to see him there in this position that,


00:55:05.688 --> 00:55:07.349
I'd never seen him in anything


00:55:07.356 --> 00:55:10.098
except being pretty much
on top of the world.


00:55:10.109 --> 00:55:11.815
- The sensational case involves


00:55:11.819 --> 00:55:14.151
more than 200 pounds of cocaine,


00:55:14.155 --> 00:55:16.146
an elaborate undercover investigation,


00:55:16.157 --> 00:55:18.148
the end of the DeLorean car,


00:55:18.159 --> 00:55:19.695
and much, much more.


00:55:25.916 --> 00:55:28.658
- It's been said, it's a cliche now,


00:55:28.669 --> 00:55:31.536
that the dream had
turned into a nightmare.


00:55:32.798 --> 00:55:34.754
Could you imagine how we felt


00:55:34.759 --> 00:55:36.624
seeing John DeLorean,


00:55:36.636 --> 00:55:39.924
our angel, I feel like?


00:55:39.930 --> 00:55:41.670
There he was going away,


00:55:41.682 --> 00:55:44.298
and everybody was downtrodden


00:55:44.310 --> 00:55:48.599
because we felt sure
that he had done so much.


00:55:48.606 --> 00:55:50.471
He'd come through again,


00:55:50.483 --> 00:55:53.316
but this time, unfortunately, he didn't.


00:55:54.820 --> 00:55:55.855
-  Well, you're
walking out of the plant


00:55:55.863 --> 00:55:56.693
for the last time today.


00:55:56.697 --> 00:55:57.527
How do you feel about it?


00:55:57.531 --> 00:55:58.361
- We got nothing.


00:55:58.366 --> 00:55:59.776
- Pretty upset.


00:55:59.784 --> 00:56:01.274
-  What're your chances
of getting another job now?


00:56:01.285 --> 00:56:02.195
- None at all.


00:56:02.203 --> 00:56:03.443
-  Not very good at all.


00:56:03.454 --> 00:56:05.786
- I had to go into the plant that morning,


00:56:05.790 --> 00:56:07.371
call the people together.


00:56:07.375 --> 00:56:09.582
They'd all heard the
same thing, of course,


00:56:10.628 --> 00:56:12.493
and it really was the end.


00:56:13.798 --> 00:56:16.335
- I was going around with a auctioneer,


00:56:16.342 --> 00:56:18.958
and he was putting tags


00:56:18.969 --> 00:56:20.334
on every piece of equipment,


00:56:21.847 --> 00:56:23.132
and that was my last day.


00:56:25.559 --> 00:56:27.595
- The expectation of everybody


00:56:27.603 --> 00:56:29.139
working their hearts out over there


00:56:29.146 --> 00:56:31.478
to get these cars built
to a good standard,


00:56:31.482 --> 00:56:34.895
and they felt it was gonna be their life,


00:56:36.987 --> 00:56:37.817
and it all fell apart,


00:56:37.822 --> 00:56:39.562
and it was incredibly sad.


00:56:43.077 --> 00:56:44.817
- I meet up with people now and then


00:56:44.829 --> 00:56:48.868
that either they worked there


00:56:48.874 --> 00:56:49.863
or their dad worked there


00:56:49.875 --> 00:56:51.991
or their granddad worked there,


00:56:52.002 --> 00:56:53.117
and you get the same questions.


00:56:53.129 --> 00:56:53.959
"What was it really like?"


00:56:53.963 --> 00:56:57.205
It was the best job that we ever had,


00:56:57.216 --> 00:56:59.207
and it was the worst day of their lives


00:56:59.218 --> 00:57:01.004
when it finally closed.


00:57:05.975 --> 00:57:07.715
-  After entering
a plea of not guilty


00:57:07.727 --> 00:57:09.809
to all nine drug charges against him,


00:57:09.812 --> 00:57:11.803
John DeLorean came out
of the federal courthouse


00:57:11.814 --> 00:57:13.520
in Los Angeles with his wife,


00:57:13.524 --> 00:57:15.139
model Cristina Ferrare,


00:57:15.151 --> 00:57:16.186
and the four lawyers


00:57:16.193 --> 00:57:18.058
who are now putting together his defense.


00:57:18.070 --> 00:57:19.856
DeLorean said nothing today,


00:57:19.864 --> 00:57:21.525
but he is reported to
have told his lawyers


00:57:21.532 --> 00:57:23.648
he was desperate to save his company.


00:57:23.659 --> 00:57:25.365
DeLorean's lawyers are preparing to argue


00:57:25.369 --> 00:57:27.860
the government entrapped DeLorean.


00:57:27.872 --> 00:57:28.782
-  Give him some room, guys.


00:57:28.789 --> 00:57:29.619
Give him some room.


00:57:29.623 --> 00:57:30.453
Come on.


00:57:30.458 --> 00:57:33.416
- It was the case of the
century for that year


00:57:34.336 --> 00:57:36.292
'cause every year has
a case of the century,


00:57:36.297 --> 00:57:38.083
trial of the century,


00:57:38.090 --> 00:57:41.674
so we were the trial of
the century for 1984.


00:57:41.677 --> 00:57:43.463
-  The sidewalks in
front of the courthouse


00:57:43.471 --> 00:57:44.961
will be jammed with reporters


00:57:44.972 --> 00:57:47.179
and camera crews from all over the world.


00:57:47.183 --> 00:57:48.764
- Unless you were there and you lived it,


00:57:48.768 --> 00:57:50.633
you don't know how captivating


00:57:50.644 --> 00:57:52.885
it was for the nation.


00:57:52.897 --> 00:57:54.228
-  A young entrepreneur started


00:57:54.231 --> 00:57:56.017
hanging around the courthouse steps today


00:57:56.025 --> 00:57:57.481
hawking buttons that say,


00:57:57.485 --> 00:57:59.976
"John 2. DeLorean Cocaine
Trafficking Trial,


00:57:59.987 --> 00:58:01.693
"Los Angeles, '84."


00:58:01.697 --> 00:58:06.191
- Our public views these
as if it's a bad guy,


00:58:06.202 --> 00:58:08.944
get them, and if it's not a bad guy,


00:58:08.954 --> 00:58:09.864
get the government.


00:58:09.872 --> 00:58:12.659
It's all really kind of
entertainment to the public.


00:58:12.666 --> 00:58:15.624
- John DeLorean had some bad news today.


00:58:16.754 --> 00:58:19.712
The Automobile Association
named him dealer of the year.


00:58:23.052 --> 00:58:24.542
- A businessman down on his luck


00:58:24.553 --> 00:58:26.418
tries to make money in a dope deal.


00:58:26.430 --> 00:58:30.389
I mean, that's the John
DeLorean that I knew.


00:58:30.392 --> 00:58:31.222
-  Okay, you know how--


00:58:31.227 --> 00:58:33.468
-  The case was
of overwhelming strength.


00:58:34.313 --> 00:58:36.019
Here's the cocaine,


00:58:36.023 --> 00:58:37.763
and here's your target,


00:58:37.775 --> 00:58:41.267
and he's holding up a glass
of champagne, toasting.


00:58:42.363 --> 00:58:44.103
- To a lot of success for everybody.


00:58:44.114 --> 00:58:46.605
- And says that this is better than,


00:58:46.617 --> 00:58:47.447
what was it?


00:58:47.451 --> 00:58:49.237
Better than something.


00:58:49.245 --> 00:58:50.280
- Is it better than gold?


00:58:51.121 --> 00:58:53.612
Gold weighs more than
that, for God's sakes.


00:58:53.624 --> 00:58:56.206
- Everybody believed that the events


00:58:56.210 --> 00:58:58.041
were showing John DeLorean


00:58:58.045 --> 00:59:00.206
in the middle of a drug deal.


00:59:00.214 --> 00:59:01.545
One of the things I asked


00:59:01.549 --> 00:59:03.505
the jurors very early on was,


00:59:03.509 --> 00:59:05.670
would it surprise you if I told you


00:59:05.678 --> 00:59:07.589
you weren't seeing a drug deal?


00:59:07.596 --> 00:59:10.383
And just hang on and
watch the whole movie,


00:59:10.391 --> 00:59:12.006
and at the end,


00:59:12.017 --> 00:59:15.680
you're gonna see it isn't
what it appeared to be,


00:59:15.688 --> 00:59:17.804
a real thriller, of course,


00:59:17.815 --> 00:59:19.100
which it turned out to be.


00:59:26.782 --> 00:59:31.651
- The case starts with a
guy named James Hoffman.


00:59:31.662 --> 00:59:34.199
He was the informant.


00:59:34.206 --> 00:59:38.370
He was somebody who had been
a major narcotics trafficker


00:59:38.377 --> 00:59:41.869
who had the bad luck of being caught,


00:59:41.881 --> 00:59:44.372
and he decided to cooperate.


00:59:44.383 --> 00:59:45.293
-  Valestra.


